Save93 equipment comparison at 42 FPS: catch-up off against accelerate with the skip rule, 2026-11-11 13:07-13:17
Test DLL APBGameplayTrace.dll SHA-256 6DF3C7534F0A5ECCDD66F4E4E493F24879ED0720B9805E5F1FA2F1E7CB6F9A76 (12:26 build; the skip rule is
the release plugin's option [VMCatchUp] SkipWaitingCalls, switched here by the test control).
Command: build/catchup/run-effect-ab.ps1 -Prefix sk42 -Limits 42 -TraceMode catchup-ab -SkipBlocked -Repeat 2
Two launches of eight trials (off,on,on,off,on,off,off,on), target 60, profile APB-CatchUp-Test, window in front 100% in all
16 trials, guard_violations 0, no "turned off" line. Run folders build/catchup/sk42-42-1, sk42-42-2.

RESULT
- Equipment drain time (the measure used at 30 FPS): no difference at 42 FPS. With 174 handlers off 249-277 ms (7 trials), on
  204-254 ms (8 trials); both sit at about 250 ms, the floor of this scene (the last handler waits about 0.2 s itself). The one
  trial with 203 handlers was an off trial (634 ms), so there is no pair for it. At 42 FPS this scene builds no backlog that
  extra cycles could remove, as in the earlier 42 FPS runs of the release rule.
- Queue wait of ALL queued native calls (not only the equipment handlers), pooled over the trials of both launches:
    off: 20,534 calls, mean wait 24.2 ms, in the same frame 9.2%, two frames or more 3.1%
    on:  20,970 calls, mean wait 17.7 ms, in the same frame 24.1%, two frames or more 1.5%
  Per trial the mean is 21.7-26.6 ms off and 17.1-18.3 ms on; the two launches agree (24.1 / 24.4 off, 17.7 / 17.7 on).
  That is 27% less waiting per queued call. At 30 FPS the same rule gave about 36 -> 22-25 ms and 9% -> 36% in the same frame.
- Extra cycles ran in 142-144 of 334-335 frames per trial, which is every chance the target of 60 gives at 42 FPS (3 in 7
  frames). 414-633 extra calls and 448-756 front skips per trial; the queue was rebuilt after 2 cycles in one trial.
- Cost: 0.046-0.058 ms per frame, at most 0.70 ms in one frame. Engine frame time p99 26.2 ms off, 26.5 ms on (first launch).
- The usual two exit crash logs after each launch.

READING
- At 42 FPS the option shortens the wait of queued calls by about a quarter, less than at 30 FPS, because fewer frames get
  an extra cycle (3 in 7 instead of every frame). Whether that is felt depends on how far scripts are behind: in this scene
  at 42 FPS they are not behind, so the time until the equipment events have drained does not change.
- Not measured here: the release rule (SkipWaitingCalls=false) at 42 FPS on this build, so the two rules cannot be set side
  by side at this frame rate; other scenes.

